From 31828ef787702fa87b9077c268cc47c8589c0c83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brent Kearney <1550934+brentkearney@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 18:29:18 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 01/11] wifi: brcmfmac: add AWDL interface creation for BCM4387

BCM4387 firmware implements AWDL (Apple Wireless Direct Link) and accepts
BRCMF_INTERFACE_TYPE_AWDL through interface_create, but nl80211 has no AWDL
iftype, so the interface cannot be created via add_virtual_intf(). Drive it
from a vendor command instead.

Creation is asynchronous, and has to be. nl80211 dispatches vendor commands
holding the wiphy mutex: NL80211_CMD_VENDOR sets NL80211_FLAG_NEED_WIPHY
without NL80211_FLAG_NO_WIPHY_MTX, unlike NL80211_CMD_NEW_INTERFACE which
sets NEED_RTNL | NO_WIPHY_MTX and takes the wiphy mutex itself, in that
order. Registering a netdev from the vendor command therefore deadlocks:

  nl80211_vendor_cmd                    <- holds wiphy mutex
   brcmf_awdl_add_vif
    brcmf_net_attach
     register_netdev                    <- takes RTNL
      register_netdevice
       call_netdevice_notifiers_info
        cfg80211_netdev_notifier_call
         mutex_lock                     <- wiphy mutex, already held

The task blocks in uninterruptible sleep while holding the RTNL, which takes
down every netdev operation on the system; recovery needs a reboot. Using
cfg80211_register_netdevice() instead is not a fix either: it requires both
the RTNL and the wiphy mutex, and the vendor command holds only the latter,
so it trips ASSERT_RTNL and registers into an inconsistent state. Taking the
RTNL explicitly would invert cfg80211's RTNL-then-wiphy order.

So brcmf_awdl_add_vif() only asks firmware for the interface and returns.
The netdev is registered later by brcmf_cfg80211_awdl_attach_pending(),
called from the fweh event worker, which runs on the system workqueue
holding neither lock. The vif event stays armed across the request because
brcmf_notify_vif_event() is what links the brcmf_if to the vif; without it
ndev->ieee80211_ptr is never set and cfg80211 ignores the netdev.

Userspace must wait for awdl0 to appear rather than assume the vendor
command created it.
